  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of the mapping found:\n\n**Babel side**\n- `packages/babel-plugin-proposal-decorators/src/index.ts` \u2014 the stage-2/stage-3 branch point: `legacy || version === \"legacy\"` routes to `transformer-legacy.ts`; otherwise it delegates to `createClassFeaturePlugin` (stage-3 TC39 form) in `@babel/helper-create-class-features-plugin`.\n- `packages/babel-plugin-proposal-decorators/src/transformer-legacy.ts` \u2014 the legacy-decorator visitor (fork of `babel-plugin-transform-decorators-legacy`). It emits `_applyDecoratedDescriptor`/`_initializerWarningHelper` calls but **never emits `Reflect.metadata`/`design:paramtypes`** \u2014 there is no `emitDecoratorMetadata`-equivalent anywhere in Babel's TypeScript pipeline (confirmed by an empty grep across the whole babel repo for `emitDecoratorMetadata`/`design:paramtypes`). This is the real root of the customer's original Babel setup working only if they were using a third-party plugin (`babel-plugin-transform-typescript-metadata`) \u2014 Babel's own `preset-typescript`/`plugin-transform-typescript` has zero type-metadata support since Babel strips types without a type checker.\n- `packages/babel-generator/src/generators/{modules,expressions}.ts` \u2014 the `decoratorsBeforeExport` print logic, relevant to the \"ordering vs preset-typescript\" interplay the task calls out.\n\n**SWC side**\n- `crates/swc/src/config/mod.rs` \u2014 wires `jsc.transform.legacyDecorator` \u2192 `legacy_decorator` and `jsc.transform.decoratorMetadata` \u2192 `decorator_metadata`, both defaulting `false` (`BoolConfig<false>`), then builds `swc_ecma_transforms_proposal::proposals::decorators::Config{ legacy, emit_metadata, .. }`.\n- `crates/swc_ecma_transforms_proposal/src/decorators/mod.rs` \u2014 the stage split: `if c.legacy` \u2192 legacy path (metadata-capable); else stage-3 `Decorators` fold, which explicitly `unimplemented!()`s if `emit_metadata` is requested \u2014 i.e., **SWC only supports `decoratorMetadata` in `legacyDecorator: true` mode**, exactly like real `tsc`.\n- `crates/swc_ecma_transforms_proposal/src/decorators/legacy/mod.rs` (+ its `metadata` submodule) \u2014 implements `ts_decorate`/`Reflect.metadata` emission by walking the **still-typed** class AST (`Metadata::new`, `ParamMetadata`) before type stripping, so this pass must run on typed AST \u2014 i.e. before/alongside `swc_ecma_transforms_typescript::typescript::strip`, not after.\n- `crates/swc_ecma_transforms_typescript/src/typescript.rs` / `tests/strip.rs` \u2014 the type-stripping pass and its test harness show `decorators(...)` chained in the same fold pipeline as `strip`, confirming the ordering constraint.\n\n**Divergence / customer's actual bug:** Under Babel, `Reflect.metadata` never existed regardless of config \u2014 DI worked only via a supplemental metadata plugin (or the customer never actually had it and NestJS DI \"worked\" only because it wasn't exercising constructor-param injection paths that need it). Under SWC, decorator metadata *is* implemented, but it's opt-in and gated on **both** `jsc.transform.legacyDecorator: true` **and** `jsc.transform.decoratorMetadata: true` \u2014 both default to `false`. If the customer's `.swcrc` only set `legacyDecorator: true` (to get `@injectable()` parsing/emission working at all) but omitted `decoratorMetadata: true`, `Decorators`/`TscDecorator` never emits the `Reflect.metadata(\"design:paramtypes\", \u2026)` call, so Nest's `Reflect.getMetadata` calls return `undefined` at DI-resolution time \u2014 matching the reported crash exactly.",
